{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-41839" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F=xyz\\,i+{e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,j+({e}^{-y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi z+2xz)\\,k.$ \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(1,0,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ffca4966bdc50947017d95a0a9be626c9f15bd4b6d9fd1f4cfacff33ca4f4d9"} {"question" "q-41839" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{-y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi z+2xz) \\\\  & =yz-{e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x+\\pi {e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi z+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(1,0,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(1,0,0) & =(0)(0)-{e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\pi {e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+2(1) \\\\  & =0-(-1)+\\pi (1)+2 \\\\  & =1+\\pi +2 \\\\  & =\\pi +3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ffca4966bdc50947017d95a0a9be626c9f15bd4b6d9fd1f4cfacff33ca4f4d9"} {"question" "q-41839" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "205a9fee92f6a5f4dd99d234461636bfe2ae9985f0386a5f9ad3a75a5c70f907"} {"question" "q-41839" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ffca4966bdc50947017d95a0a9be626c9f15bd4b6d9fd1f4cfacff33ca4f4d9"} {"question" "q-41839" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "1-\\pi "] ["math" "3-\\pi "] ["math" "\\pi +1"] ["math" "\\pi +3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ffca4966bdc50947017d95a0a9be626c9f15bd4b6d9fd1f4cfacff33ca4f4d9"} {"question" "q-41839" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\pi +3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ffca4966bdc50947017d95a0a9be626c9f15bd4b6d9fd1f4cfacff33ca4f4d9"} {"question" "q-105573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/assets/3088d088689bbd0dfd33abe8240c6f02cceb257937fc1fccb8ba3df151dbfaab.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $\\text{div}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F>0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ffca4966bdc50947017d95a0a9be626c9f15bd4b6d9fd1f4cfacff33ca4f4d9"} {"question" "q-105573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/assets/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statements I and III are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $y$-components increase as we move vertically away from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ffca4966bdc50947017d95a0a9be626c9f15bd4b6d9fd1f4cfacff33ca4f4d9"} {"question" "q-105573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "205a9fee92f6a5f4dd99d234461636bfe2ae9985f0386a5f9ad3a75a5c70f907"} {"question" "q-105573" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ffca4966bdc50947017d95a0a9be626c9f15bd4b6d9fd1f4cfacff33ca4f4d9"} {"question" "q-105573" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and IV only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and IV only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ffca4966bdc50947017d95a0a9be626c9f15bd4b6d9fd1f4cfacff33ca4f4d9"} {"question" "q-105573" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ffca4966bdc50947017d95a0a9be626c9f15bd4b6d9fd1f4cfacff33ca4f4d9"} {"question" "q-105576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/assets/cf932f4f02a216d5111da0748cb04f815a7d2e85af5db2f9282f5aefc359b816.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=0$\n- $∇\\cdot F>0$\n- $∇\\cdot F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ffca4966bdc50947017d95a0a9be626c9f15bd4b6d9fd1f4cfacff33ca4f4d9"} {"question" "q-105576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/assets/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere, the vector field $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ffca4966bdc50947017d95a0a9be626c9f15bd4b6d9fd1f4cfacff33ca4f4d9"} {"question" "q-105576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "205a9fee92f6a5f4dd99d234461636bfe2ae9985f0386a5f9ad3a75a5c70f907"} {"question" "q-105576" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ffca4966bdc50947017d95a0a9be626c9f15bd4b6d9fd1f4cfacff33ca4f4d9"} {"question" "q-105576" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and IV only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and IV only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "IV only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ffca4966bdc50947017d95a0a9be626c9f15bd4b6d9fd1f4cfacff33ca4f4d9"} {"question" "q-105576" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and IV only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ffca4966bdc50947017d95a0a9be626c9f15bd4b6d9fd1f4cfacff33ca4f4d9"} {"question" "q-123200" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F=(x-y)^{3}\\,i+(y+z)^{3}\\,j+(x+z)^{3}\\,k.$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,2,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ffca4966bdc50947017d95a0a9be626c9f15bd4b6d9fd1f4cfacff33ca4f4d9"} {"question" "q-123200" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-y)^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y+z)^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}((x+z)^{3}) \\\\  & =3(x-y)^{2}+3(y+z)^{2}+3(x+z)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,2,2),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F(-2,2,2) & =3((-2)-(2))^{2}+3((2)+(2))^{2}+3((-2)+(2))^{2} \\\\  & =3(-4)^{2}+3(4)^{2}+3(0)^{2} \\\\  & =96.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ffca4966bdc50947017d95a0a9be626c9f15bd4b6d9fd1f4cfacff33ca4f4d9"} {"question" "q-123200" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "va